ind  that the fix isn't already in floor  yannelli says  luck plays of course a huge role but  let's not forget that who gets lucky  highly depends on the people deciding so  this is why diverse selection panels are  so important it's not just a matter of  representation but also of reducing bias  a point that the  original poster made  going on arsenal on khan says agree with  all of this except that the system  selects randomly mostly they select from  institutions deemed prestigious but even  then luck plays a big role  melanated scholar following up on this  yep and sometimes you gotta get real  shiny mit made me glitter in the eyes of  academia well if mit likes her we should  to  write and then dr bradley summer says  the other thing i would add is have an  ivy league degree although that usually  implies a certain amount of luck too  mostly in the form of having rich  parents and or being legacy so yeah luck  and ivy league and these three are  pointing out that prestige is indeed  another factor an arbitrary factor and  often itself does depend on luck if we  go on to the next three feed me  scanlines right on the money lucky to be  born in a place and time be born to  certain parents lucky to meet certain  people during your life lucky you were  in the right place at the right time  everybody works hard not everybody gets  lucky scott cunningham says tell parents  to be lucky when they were growing up we  often underrate the importance of where  you're coming from  sean hagen says i would say knowing the  right people helps but that's also  basically just luck so what we're seeing  here is that luck comprises a lot more  than just a purely random flip the coin  roll the dice hit the random number  generator sort of factor following up on  this dr rebecca rooney says threads  recently on the role of luck in  academia's tenure track job market in  these luck and merit are like alternate  drivers of success like don't believe in  the meritocracy being meritorious isn't  enough you mostly need luck this ignores  a key factor privilege a third important  factor that many others are highlighting  and that the original poster addressed  marina the hydrologist many professors  who made it think they know what it  takes but it's just survivor bias for  every one of them that made it with  those tricks there are a hundred who  didn't racism and sexism are real  barriers to getting an academic job that  are never acknowledged even in this  thread of course he did acknowledge it  in the thread but it's it's quite true  um there are lots of factors that stand  in the way in many fields aaron carrillo  says have white cis-het male  neurotypical non-disabled privilege and  make it clear you will maintain the  status quo  rather cynical there but uh there's  there's some measure of truth to that  depending on what job we're looking at  and what institution kelly wilts says i  applied for the job i have three times  and didn't even get a phone interview  why because senior faculty in the  department had a narrow view of what  they wanted we're trying to hire  research one folks for a regional  teaching university and kept hiring  people who didn't care about teaching a  very very common hiring mistake that  gets made and i've seen it happen myself  in place after place after place those  those research one folks don't stick  around quite frankly uh and then kelly  follows up saying racist and sexist  faculty won't hire anyone who does edi  work for fear of being called on their  behavior to anyone on the market right  now your rejection has little to do with  your qualifications and everything to do  with the senior faculty who should have  retired decades ago and i think that  there is a very important point to that  because  of the financial crisis that started in  the  early aughts a lot of older faculty who  should have retired didn't retire and  their positions you know just got held  onto many of them hanging on for another  decade and controlling the hiring  decisions here's the last one and i  think this is an important point as well  i've seen this happen to many colleagues  carla karen says this is so real my  sister spent 16 years as a visiting  professor than adjunct because she  married a colleague at least she had  health insurance as a faculty spouse but  despite her scholarship teaching and  service for years she was never  considered when they had tenure track  openings dear student responds that's  the weird thing no one looks to  non-tenured to promote to tenure track  the system is broken when you can't  value loyalty and hard work that's  already teaching in your program this  has been a problem for  decades and it's a reversal of how  things  used to be  a long time ago where teaching as an  adjunct would in fact give you a leg up  in applying for a place in  that school except for certain elite  institutions  so that's that's a very important  consideration as well so a lot to say  about this and i think it's great that  people are engaged in open discussion  about these matters a lot of people in  academia or who leave academia suffer  considerably because they  think that the problem is with them when  really it's a chance driven and largely  unfair system it's if it ever was a  meritocracy it isn't anymore and this is  perhaps even more the case at elite  schools where you just look at at the  people that are there many of them are  very smart a lot of them are just  placeholders but happened to  ticked off the right boxes for hiring  committees and i don't think that its  talent pool is different at elite  universities than it is at most other  places so we have a lot of very  interesting points to consider  in this twitter conversation  you
 

